+# Testing boundaries and proof
+
+## Boundary and cost
+
+Prove a caller-visible contract through its production owner, with fixed external I/O fixtures and independent expected values. Prefer focused integration proof or an independent unit contract to mocked internal decisions or whole-product boots. Name the credible regression and what the test cannot prove.
+
+For a regression, reproduce the original defect, repair its owner, and rerun the proof. The pre-fix failure must come from the defect, not fixture setup; report when baseline proof is unavailable. Preserve distinct contracts when consolidating coverage. Behavior-preserving refactors should keep tests green.
+
+Use synthetic content and keep credentials out of fixtures and captures. Isolate state, ports and clocks with the repository’s existing helpers. Avoid real sleeps and polling where controlled time can prove the same contract. Fix leaked state instead of hiding it with serial execution or retries.
+
+Use the repository’s focused test command and cost budgets. Measure new or materially changed test cost when relevant to the change; choose the cheapest layer that still proves the contract. Keep live, whole-product and release proof separate where they exercise distinct risks.
+
+## Proof policy
+
+- Read the owning repository’s root and scoped instructions. Use its test wrappers, changed-file gates and required review; this reference adds no permission to publish, provision remote machines, contact live services or run an external review.
+- Prove the changed contract through its real entry point and relevant sibling behavior. Complete required targeted type, lint and format checks. Broaden or repeat only for changed inputs, failures or a named unresolved risk.
+- Where the authorized delivery path includes CI, use its required broad proof. Do not rerun or re-push merely to obtain green; diagnose failures against the actual revision and job.
+- State unavailable OS, device, credentialed or live-provider proof as a gap. Local, mocked or fixture proof establishes only the boundary exercised.
+- Preserve active writers, running tests, operator processes and real data. Use isolated task-owned fixtures and clean them up. Do not edit a checkout while its tests are running.
+- For documentation-only changes, use relevant link, format and diff checks; do not invent runtime testing.
+- Complete the repository’s required review and delivery only within existing task authority. No commit, push or landing permission is implied by this skill.
+
+## Provenance
+
+Portable excerpts adapted from OpenClaw’s [writing tests guide](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/71b5563397e95d67ad902f79a717559519010ac8/docs/help/testing/writing-tests.md) and [testing proof policy](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/71b5563397e95d67ad902f79a717559519010ac8/.agents/skills/openclaw-testing/SKILL.md#proof-policy). Repository-specific commands, infrastructure and budgets remain with the repository being changed.
